<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of al-Uqhuwana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1029">1029</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of al-Uqhuwana was fought in 1029 near Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, where a Fatimid army under Anushtakin al-Dizbari defeated an alliance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrahids">Jarrahids</a> and the Mirdasids. The Mirdasid emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salih-ibn-mirdas">Salih ibn Mirdas</a> was killed. The victory restored Fatimid control over Palestine and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and broke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> plan to divide the region.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Faction fighting at the Fatimid court after the disappearance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Hakim in 1021 and a severe famine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1023 encouraged the three largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> to seek power. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kilab">Kilab</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salih-ibn-mirdas">Salih ibn Mirdas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayy">Tayy</a> under the Jarrahid Hassan ibn al-Mufarrij and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kalb">Kalb</a> under Sinan ibn Ulayyan agreed to divide Syria: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> as far as al-Arish for the Tayy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> for the Kalb and the land between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anah">Anah</a> for the Kilab.</p><p>In September 1024 a tax quarrel between Hassan and the Fatimid governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, Anushtakin al-Dizbari, became open revolt. Hassan's forces plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a>, and he later sacked and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a>, executing much of its garrison and enslaving many women and children. By 1026, when Anushtakin was recalled, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrahids">Jarrahids</a> dominated Palestine and the Mirdasids had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The vizier Ali al-Jarjara'i sent Anushtakin back in late 1028 with an army of seven thousand cavalry and infantry, and asked what he needed. Anushtakin answered that he wanted only his mare, al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barda">Barda</a>'iyya, and a tent for shade. Sinan had died in mid-1028, and his nephew Rafi ibn Abi'l-Layl took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kalb">Kalb</a> over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>, joined by thousands of other loyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a>, mainly of the Fazara.</p><p>The armies met east of Lake Tiberias, near where the Jordan enters it, on 12 or 29 May 1029. Hassan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayy">Tayy</a> deserted the field, through treachery or cowardice in the view of the historian Suhayl Zakkar, and their flight decided the battle. Salih fought on until, exhausted, he dismounted to rest his horse. Fazara tribesmen under Rafi caught him and beheaded him, and his youngest son and his Christian vizier, Tadharus ibn al-Hasan, were also killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Anushtakin made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> his headquarters, and Salih's governors abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalbek">Baalbek</a>, Hisn Ibn Akkar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> and Rafaniyya to Fatimid rule. He received a string of honorific titles, among them al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> al-Muzaffar, the Victorious General. The poet al-Ma'arri lamented Salih in verse. Salih's son Nasr survived to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> until he was killed and Anushtakin took the city in 1038, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayy">Tayy</a> withdrew to the hills of Transjordan and never regained their footing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of al-Uqhuwana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Hassan joined the Mirdasids and the Kalb in a 1024 alliance to partition Syria, but the Fatimids defeated it at <b>al-Uqhuwana</b> in 1029.</blockquote><a href="/state/jarrahids">Jarrahids</a></li><li><blockquote>Salih was killed in 1029 at <b>al-Uqhuwana</b>, but his son Shibl al-Dawla Nasr defeated the Byzantine emperor Romanos III near Azaz in 1030 and then accepted Byzantine protection.</blockquote><a href="/state/mirdasid-emirate">Mirdasid Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
